These properties will be checked with Crucible, a coverage-guided fuzzer for Solana programs.

**How Crucible executes them, and what that means for how you write them.** Crucible drives *random
sequences* of the program's instructions and evaluates every property after every action. So the properties you
write must hold across ANY reachable sequence of actions, not only within a single instruction — and the most
valuable ones are exactly those a *sequence* can violate: conservation of value, monotonicity, "this can only ever
happen once", access control that must survive whatever came before. Prefer those over properties local to a single
call, and phrase each as something checkable against on-chain state after an arbitrary action. Note that the whole
program's instructions interleave in that sequence — including ones outside the capability you are reviewing.

As a fuzzer it cannot *prove* universally quantified properties or invariants, but it approximates them well and any
*refutation* (a fuzzing counterexample / crash) is extremely valuable. So state universal safety properties and
invariants freely — do not restrict yourself because a fuzzer cannot definitively prove them.

**What the checker can see.** Each property becomes a predicate over on-chain account state, evaluated *between*
actions. It cannot see which instruction just ran, cannot compare against the state before it, and cannot see inside
a transaction. Four rules follow, and they are the difference between a property that gets checked and one that is
later declined as unformalizable:

- **A per-call delta is not checkable; the standing relation it protects usually is.** "After switching elevation
  group the obligation is stale", "a second refresh in the same slot changes nothing", "this instruction must not
  lower that field" — none can be observed, because every mutating instruction leaves similar marks and no earlier
  value is retained. State instead what must hold *at all times* and that the bad change would break: "an obligation
  marked fresh has every reserve it references fresh for the current slot". Where only the per-call form says what
  you mean, write it, and name in the same description the standing relation you would settle for.

- **Name the account fields that decide it.** A violation is detectable exactly when it shows in a field of some
  account. A bonus rate the program computes and never stores, a fee that lands in an accumulator four other
  instructions also feed — these leave nothing to read afterwards. Name the fields a checker would compare; if the
  deciding quantity is never persisted, say so in the description rather than leaving it to be discovered later.

- **"Must be rejected" needs the exact attempt.** The test harness is built from these properties, so a rejection
  property that names the concrete attempt — who calls what, with which account substituted or which precondition
  violated — becomes an action that makes the attempt and records whether it was accepted. One that says only "must
  fail" becomes nothing, because a correctly rejected instruction and one that was never sent leave identical state.
  Name the attempt, and name what would be observable afterwards if it wrongly succeeded. The same applies to
  liveness ("this call must succeed for its rightful owner"): that is refuted by a *revert*, which leaves no trace
  unless the attempt is named.

- **Constrain what the program writes, not what the harness writes.** A property over values only the test harness
  ever supplies — raw oracle account bytes, a config field no instruction sets, a bound the harness's own parameter
  ranges already keep — holds under every possible implementation, including a badly broken one, so it certifies
  nothing. Prefer the constraint on what the *program* derives from those inputs.

Two consequences worth knowing rather than working around. A Rust panic and a returned `Err` are indistinguishable
afterwards — both roll the transaction back, leaving byte-identical state — so phrase an abort/DoS property as the
instruction failing, and put the panic mechanism in the description rather than in the checkable claim. And a value a
transaction perturbs and restores before it ends (a flash borrow's dip in available liquidity) is never visible to a
between-actions observer; if that excursion is the property, say what a *second* instruction spliced into the window
would leave behind.

A few categories are a poor fit and should be skipped: properties about off-chain events (key compromise, social
engineering, oracle manipulation outside the modeled accounts), and pure hash-collision resistance ("no two inputs ever
collide"), which sampling cannot refute. Arithmetic-overflow and type-level facts are worth stating: Rust overflow
panics and Anchor constraint failures surface as crashes the fuzzer can find.
